<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Chronicleaf.Capture</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Chronicleaf.Capture</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The design names two files in this project - the Playwright provider and the sandbox - and names
      the `IScreenshotProvider` seam in Chronicleaf.Application. Those two facts together fix the
      direction of this reference: the provider implements an interface it cannot see otherwise, so this
      project points at the product rather than the other way round, and Chronicleaf.Application knows
      nothing about Playwright. That is what keeps the seam narrow - a test substitutes the interface,
      and the browser driver is only ever loaded by the composition root.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Application/Chronicleaf.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Core/Chronicleaf.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The browser driver, pinned so a restore is reproducible: the Chromium build Playwright ships is
      resolved and matched at runtime by the package itself, never by whatever happens to be in
      ~/.cache on the machine. This is the one native dependency this package adds, and the design
      requires it to be provisioned in the container image rather than found by searching PATH.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.51.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What this project deliberately cannot reach is as important as what it can.

    There is no reference to Chronicleaf.Infrastructure, so nothing here resolves a media key, a
    connection string or a storage provider - a capture takes bytes, and the delivered media store
    writes them. There is no reference to the credential vault, so a browser started from here has no
    path to a token and therefore no way to photograph a logged-in page: the isolated context this
    package requires (no saved cookies, no login, no secrets) is structural rather than a setting
    somebody could turn off.
  -->

</Project>
